            <div xml:id="d241">

                <pb facs="0664" n="614" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv12/pg614"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conf. 184.011102/203: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Albert Halstead
                        to the Commission to Negotiate Peace</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-07-24T17:50:00+01:00">July 24, 1919—5:50
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 25—6:10 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>654. Referring to my telegram No. 646.<anchor n="79" xml:id="d241-f1"/> Colonel
                    Cunningham saw Boehm today. Said he was consulting his friends and would be
                    ready to act in about a month and that it would require 48 hours to overthrow
                    Kun regime, but he could not proceed until assured that Allies would approve
                    plan outlined in my telegram 646. In view of your telegram number 137<anchor
                        n="79" xml:id="d241-f2"/> I cannot recommend approval of plan which was
                    entirely subject to approval of governments concerned but can only report facts.
                    Respectfully inquire whether raising of blockade and furnishing food and raw
                    materials as means of improving <pb facs="0665" n="615" xml:id="pg615"/>
                    conditions and destroying Bolsheviki terror, but I fear their presence would be
                    considered partial recognition and strengthen Bela Kun. The other Allied
                    representatives agree with this view.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Halstead</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
